ying c6 after we play see for  Black has the option of playing e6 which  is the Icelandic gamete and after white  captures on e6 black can recapture on e6  with his Bishop and as you can see black  is down upon but all his pieces are very  active he has a developed Knight he has  a developed Bishop to e6 here and his  Queen has this open file he's attacking  this pawn on c4 and all his pieces look  very active where white really doesn't  look like he has many active pieces he  has a random pawn on c4 but none of his  pieces have moved anywhere this is  actually my favorite the Icelandic  gambit just because I like to play very  aggressive and this is a very aggressive  defense for black so that's what you may  see if they start out with c4 and they  try to hold on to it the white may not  always try to hold on to it the actual  main line is for white to play d4 giving  back this pawn on d5 knowing that if he  doesn't play before then black can get a  lot of spatial and development advantage  so many times you'll  see wipe lady for giving back this pawn  on d5 from here if black takes on d5  regaining them material back White has a  few options the first option is two  ideas one would be to place c4 first and  then black and choose if he wants to  play Knight to f6 which is very passive  you never really want to move your  knight back to the same square that came  from it's kind of a wasted move a lot of  times you'll see Knight to b6 as you can  see we're already starting to put a lot  of pressure on black that's one idea  another idea white may have is to first  play Knight to f3 and then when Bishop  plays Bishop to g4 pinning our knight  here then we can come in with our pawn  to c4 it looks better for black he has  his Bishop developed but now when he  plays Knight to b6 we can actually  spring a trap on him and play pawn to c5  here now he has three options very very  passive would be to put his night on c8  now his Knights aren't doing anything he  can also put it as knight on d7 and d5  d5 looks a lot more active than d7 but  if he does play d5 we can bring our  Queen to b3 and this is actually going  to be very good for us we're attacking  this  Pease here on d5 and at the same time  we're attacking the b7 square which is  our main target here  so this will be very good for white and  even if black plays Knight to d7 as you  can see black is already starting to  cramp his style and we can continue our  development we can even bring our Bishop  to e2 and then we can Castle kingside  but this is going to be completely fine  for white so another thing you may see  instead of Knight f6 is going to be the  main line and that is with Queen  capturing on d5 you're going to see this  most times than not from here the most  common move for white and what I  recommend is Knight to c3 and this says  two things one it develops your Knights  of c3 and they gains a tempo meaning  that black is going to have to move his  Queen because right now he's being  attacked and you never really want to  move one of your minor pieces or your  major pieces more than once in the  beginning if you don't have to obviously  Black has two right now since we're  attacking it but Black has a few moves  here his worst move I would say out of  the three that people play would be  Queen to d8 this is just a super passive  move why move your queen back to the  same square that it came from more  common that you'll see is a move like  Queen to d6 which is the Bronstein  variation or the main line is going to  be Queen to a5 it's a very active square  for the Queen if you're going to bring  your queen out more than likely you want  to keep them active no point putting him  back on d8 since that's where it came  from  there's nothing white can do right now  to kick this Queen out of the way one of  the key things that you want to think  about when you're white or black a lot  of beginners always have the thought in  their head if  is out they have to try to 